  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1151 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThe current surface analysis displays high pressure positioned \nover the western Great Lakes, delivering clear skies and pleasant \nfall conditions across northern Wisconsin. On the western flank of\nthe ridge, warm advection is increasing across the Northern \nPlains ahead of a shortwave trough advancing through central \nCanada. Zonal upper-level flow will prevail through the week, \nkeeping the storm track generally north of Wisconsin with \ntemperature swings and limited moisture return.\n\nLight Rain Showers Northern Wisconsin Tonight: As the surface \nhigh slides east tonight, low-level moisture transport will \nincrease across the Upper Mississippi Valley. Moisture convergence\non the nose of a 30-knot low-level jet will support scattered \nlight rain showers moving into north- central Wisconsin late this \nevening and clearing by Tuesday sunrise. NBM guidance indicates a\n40 to 60 percent probability of 12-hour precipitation exceeding \n0.01 inches at Rhinelander and Land O Lakes, but only a 25 to 35 \npercent chance of reaching 0.10 inches. High-based cloud ceilings \nbetween 5,000 and 7,000 feet will prevent significant visibility \nreductions as showers shift eastward into Michigan.\n\nBreezy Tuesday Winds: A tightening surface pressure gradient \nahead of the approaching cold front will drive strong south to \nsouthwest winds on Tuesday, with inland daytime highs reaching the\nlower to mid-70s. Deep boundary layer mixing will support gusty \nwinds of 30 to 35 mph across central and eastern Wisconsin during \nTuesday afternoon.\n\nLate Week High Pressure Patchy Frost: Canadian surface high \npressure building across the Great Lakes Thursday and Friday will \nreinforce a cool, dry airmass and maintain dry conditions. Strong \nradiational cooling beneath clear skies and light winds Thursday\nnight and Friday night will drop overnight minimum temperatures \ninto the mid to upper 30s across north-central Wisconsin. NBM \nprobabilistic guidance shows a 30 to 50 percent probability of \ntemperatures falling to 36 degrees or colder in low-lying spots \nnear Eagle River, Rhinelander, and Crandon each night. \nTemperatures will rebound into the upper 60s and lower 70s over \nthe weekend as southerly return flow re-establishes, with low-end \nrain chances of 15 to 25 percent returning late Sunday.",
